course: biology
lesson: 208
title: Hooves, riding and caring for a horse, plus donkeys and mules
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A horse stands on just one big toe on each leg, covered by a hoof that is like a very thick fingernail.
explain: When a farrier, a person who cares for horses' feet, nails a metal shoe onto it, the horse does not mind, just as you do not feel it when your nails are cut, because the outer hoof wall has no feeling.
explain: Underneath the hoof is a soft V-shaped part called the frog, which soaks up the bumps and helps pump blood back up the leg, so riders pick out stones from around it before and after every ride.
explain: Caring for a horse means brushing it, checking its feet, keeping it warm enough and remembering that it is a flight animal that can jump sideways at a flapping bag, which is why riders always wear a helmet.
fact: A horse's hoof wall is like a thick fingernail with no feeling, so a farrier can nail on a shoe without hurting it; hooves keep growing and need trimming every few weeks.
fact: The soft V-shaped part under the hoof is called the frog; it absorbs shock and helps push blood back up the leg, and a stone stuck beside it can make a horse lame, so that it limps.
fact: Riders wear helmets on every horse, even a quiet pony, because any horse may spook (suddenly take fright) at a bag or a bird and throw its rider, and helmets prevent many serious head injuries.
fact: The four main paces, called gaits, are walk, trot, canter and gallop, from slowest to fastest, each with its own pattern of legs.
fact: In 1878 the photographer Eadweard Muybridge used a row of cameras to prove that a galloping horse has a moment with all four feet off the ground.
fact: Grooming cleans the coat and lets the rider check for cuts; dirt left under the saddle can rub sores, and horses groom each other by nibbling at the withers, the bony top of the shoulders.
fact: Some horses need rugs in winter, especially clipped or thin-coated ones, while hardy ponies grow thick coats and may need no rug at all.
fact: Horses cannot be sick, because a strong muscle at the entrance of the stomach lets food go only one way, so a tummy ache called colic can be very dangerous for a horse.
fact: Horses are measured in hands, about ten centimetres each, from the ground to the top of the shoulders; people once measured with the width of a hand.
fact: Horses are flight animals that react first and think later, so they may snort and jump at a plastic bag, and calm, patient riders help them get used to scary things.
fact: Donkeys are relatives of horses from dry desert lands, where a loud bray carries far; when worried, a donkey tends to freeze and think rather than run, which people call stubborn.
fact: A mule is the baby of a male donkey and a female horse; mules are strong and sure-footed, but they almost never have babies of their own.
exercise: Why doesn't nailing on a horseshoe hurt the horse? | The hoof wall is like a thick fingernail with no feeling | The farrier gives the horse a sleeping pill before he starts the work | The nails are made of soft rubber | Horses feel no pain anywhere
exercise: How often do hooves usually need trimming? | Every few weeks | Once in the horse's whole life | Every day after breakfast | Never, they stop growing
exercise: What is the frog of a hoof? | A soft V-shaped part under the hoof | A small animal that lives in stables | The metal shoe nailed onto the hoof wall | The long hair that grows just above the hoof
exercise: What does the frog do? | It soaks up shock and helps pump blood up the leg | It helps the horse taste the grass before it eats any of it | It grows into a new hoof each year | It stores water for the horse
exercise: Why do riders pick out the hooves? | A stuck stone can make a horse lame | It makes the hooves nice and shiny for shows | Horses cannot walk on hooves that are clean | It stops the hooves from growing too long
exercise: Why must a rider wear a helmet even on a quiet pony? | Any horse may spook and throw its rider | Quiet ponies like to bite heads | Helmets keep riders warm on cold winter mornings in the field | Helmets help the pony see the rider
exercise: Which list shows the four main gaits from slowest to fastest? | Walk, trot, canter, gallop | Trot, walk, gallop, canter | Gallop, canter, trot, walk | Walk, canter, trot, gallop
exercise: What did Muybridge's cameras prove in 1878? | A galloping horse has a moment with all four feet off the ground | A horse always keeps two feet on the ground, even at its very fastest gallop | Horses gallop on three legs only | Horses cannot run faster than people
exercise: Why should a horse be brushed before it is saddled? | Dirt under the saddle can rub sores | Brushing makes the horse run much faster | Horses refuse to move if they are dusty | Brushing changes the colour of the coat
exercise: Where do horses groom each other? | At the withers, the top of the shoulders | On the hooves | Inside each other's long ears, where flies like to sit | On the tip of the nose
exercise: Which horses are most likely to need a rug in winter? | Clipped or thin-coated horses | Hardy ponies with thick, shaggy coats | Every single horse, all year round | No horse ever needs a rug at all
exercise: Why is colic so dangerous for a horse? | A horse cannot be sick to get rid of what upsets its stomach | Colic makes horses fly into a rage and kick down their stables | Colic spreads to every horse at once | Colic stops a horse growing hair
exercise: How big is a hand when measuring a horse? | About ten centimetres | About one metre | About one centimetre | The size of the rider's hand, whatever it is
exercise: Where is a horse's height measured to? | The top of the shoulders | The tip of the ears | The top of the head | The end of the tail
exercise: Why does a horse snort and jump at a plastic bag? | It is a flight animal that reacts first and thinks later | It is trying to eat the bag because it smells of food from the shop | It is angry with the rider | It thinks the bag is a carrot
exercise: Why do people call donkeys stubborn? | When worried, they freeze and think instead of running | They hate all people | They cannot hear any commands because their ears are too long | They are always too tired to walk
exercise: What are a mule's parents? | A donkey and a horse | A horse and a cow | Two donkeys | A pony and a zebra
exercise: Why do farmers like mules? | They are strong and sure-footed | They lay eggs | They give lots of milk | They never need to eat
test: «does the horse feel it when they hammer the nails in?? sounds horrible» What is right? | No, the nails go into the hoof wall, which has no feeling, like a nail clipping | Yes, it hurts a lot, but horses are brave and never show it | Yes, that is why horses neigh | No, because farriers always use strong glue, never any nails at all, on a hoof
test: «4 horses need new shoes all round. how many shoes» | 16 | 8 | 4 | 12
test: «2 mules, a donkey n 1 horse in the stable, count the tails» | 4 | 3 | 8 | 2
test: «it's a dead quiet pony, I don't need a helmet, stop nagging» What is right? | Any pony may spook and throw you, so wear a helmet every time | Quiet ponies never move at all, so no helmet is ever needed on them | Helmets are only for racing | Only adults need helmets
test: In a cartoon a horse gallops with two legs always touching the ground like a rocking horse. What did a photographer prove in 1878? | At one moment all four feet are off the ground | A horse always keeps all four feet down | A horse gallops on its back legs only | Horses cannot gallop at all
test: «horse ate summat bad, it'll just be sick and feel better» What is right? | Horses cannot be sick, so a bad tummy ache can be very dangerous | Horses are sick every day | Horses are sick through their nose, so the bad food comes straight out | Horses do not get tummy aches
test: «pony's 12 hands, that's like 12 of my hands?» What does 12 hands roughly mean? | About 120 centimetres to the top of the shoulders | 12 metres tall | The pony is 12 years old | 12 of your hands measured from the ground to the ears
test: What would happen if a rider never picked the stones out of a horse's hooves? | A stone near the frog could make the horse lame | The hooves would stop growing | The horse would learn to fly | Nothing at all, because the stones help to keep the hooves hard
test: «stupid horse, scared of a carrier bag, what a wimp» How could a rider help? | Stay calm and let it get used to the bag slowly | Hit it until it is braver | Tie the bag to its tail so it gets used to it quickly | Shout at the horse every time
test: In Shrek, Donkey never stops talking and runs from danger. What is a real donkey more likely to do when worried? | Freeze and think before moving | Run away screaming like a horse | Talk to the nearest person about it | Fly up and over the danger like a bird
test: «two mules, so they'll have baby mules next spring yeah» What is right? | Mules almost never have babies of their own | Yes, mules have twins every year | Only white mules can have babies | Mules have babies that are horses
test: «why's my horse got no rug and my mate's has one» Which is the best reason? | A hardy pony with a thick coat may need none, a clipped horse may need one | Rugs are only for show horses | Horses without rugs are always ill | Rugs make all horses slower, so racing horses never wear them, even in the coldest winter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the attachment of a horseshoe? | Nails are driven into the hoof wall, which is insensible in the manner of a fingernail | The procedure is invariably painful to the animal | The shoe is affixed to the soft frog | Horseshoes are attached by means of the animal's own teeth
exam: Identify the principal reason for which colic constitutes a grave danger to the horse. | The animal is unable to vomit, owing to a muscle permitting the passage of food in one direction only | Colic invariably renders the animal blind | Colic is a condition peculiar to the hooves | The horse vomits continuously in consequence, losing the greater part of its body water within a few hours
exam: It is given that the height of a horse is recorded as fifteen hands. Ascertain the approximate height to the summit of its shoulders. | One hundred and fifty centimetres | Fifteen metres | Fifteen centimetres | One hundred and fifty metres
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the mule. | It is the offspring of a donkey and a horse, being strong yet almost invariably unable to breed | It is a young donkey of either sex | It is the offspring of two horses | It reproduces more readily than either parent
